hook_entries already holds the new entry (:204), so the rerun at :228 patches with both passes and leaves one set. Also port #709 change 2: in mirror_cuda_memcpy_from_symbol's fallback (nv_attach_impl_frida_setup.cpp:787-800) try *name, then boost::core::demangle(*name) when it differs. Is #709 right? Its diagnosis matches the code and the Orin data, and change 1 fixes this victim (both attaches finish before main: nothing in flight, nothing yet written to set A). Only its description is available locally (pr_709.txt has no diff), so this is reconstructed. As described it lacks the quiesce before cuModuleUnload (harmless at start-up, matters for auto-refresh or dynamic attach), recompiles everything per extra link because patch_cache/ptx_pool/module_pool are cleared, drops symbol_address_to_fatbin/current_fatbin (registration-hook records; not reachable in LD_PRELOAD mode today, only for fatbins registered after agent init, e.g. dlopen), and does not carry device-global values already written into set A over to B (only matters when the app writes globals between two attaches; any late bootstrap at runtime has that limitation already). Lower-blast-radius alternative if unloading is unwanted: walk fatbin_records in reverse in both mirror fallbacks and clear patched_global_by_name in the rerun branch (no unload; stale set A stays resident; relies on prefill keeping its front-to-back order).

In nv_attach_impl::bootstrap_existing_fatbins() (nv_attach_impl.cpp:1814; same in master), track the byte ranges already ingested in this scan and skip wrappers inside them:
  std::vector<std::pair<uintptr_t,uintptr_t>> covered;
  auto covered_by=[&](const void*p){auto a=(uintptr_t)p; return std::any_of(covered.begin(),covered.end(),[a](auto r){return a>=r.first&&a<r.second;});};
- After read_fatbin_bytes_from_ptr(sec_addr) succeeds: covered.emplace_back((uintptr_t)sec_addr,(uintptr_t)sec_addr+bytes->size());
- In the wrapper loop: if (w==nullptr || covered_by(w->data)) continue; and after a successful read, add [w->data, w->data+bytes->size()).
Result: vec_add/vec_add_legacy go from 2 ingestions to 1, the kernel_trace/cudagraph victims from 3 to 1 per round, and padded multi-TU libraries (libcurand) are unchanged.

Smallest fix, host side only (bpf_attach_ctx_cuda.cpp:121-277): after serving a request, keep polling for a short spin window before going back to the 1 ms sleep, e.g.
```
auto last = steady_clock::time_point{};
while (!stop) {
  if (__atomic_load_n(&mem->flag1, __ATOMIC_ACQUIRE) == 1) {
    mem->flag1 = 0; /* serve as today */
    __atomic_store_n(&mem->flag2, 1, __ATOMIC_RELEASE);
    last = steady_clock::now(); continue;
  }
  if (steady_clock::now() - last < 200us) { asm volatile("yield" ::: "memory"); continue; }
  sleep_for(1ms);
}
```
The window can be made configurable, e.g. BPFTIME_CUDA_WATCHER_SPIN_US.

Only moving the sleep into the idle branch is NOT enough. The next lane posts flag1 only after it has seen flag2 and taken the lock, so the immediate re-check usually finds flag1 == 0 and the watcher sleeps again.

Expected cost per call: ~5-15 us (~5 us transport plus helper work and logging). The first call of a burst still costs up to 1.06 ms. Predicted medians: cuda-counter ~1.5-2.5 ms (now 106 ms), host_map_test ~1.1-1.3 ms (now 21.3 ms), threadscheduling ~1.2-1.6 ms (now 31.8-42.3 ms). Kernels with many threads need a follow-up: per-warp slots drained in one pass, or printk on the device through the GPU ringbuf, together with the payload-race fix in other_observations.
